82421875, -0.01206207275390625, 0.01342010498046875, -0.01263427734375, 0.031768798828125, 0.036376953125, 0.0282135009765625, 0.00417327880859375, 0.00905609130859375, -0.0052642822265625, 0.018707275390625, 0.047027587890625, -0.04620361328125, -0.008209228515625, -0.042724609375, -0.046630859375, 0.00867462158203125, -0.00628662109375, 0.005634307861328125, -0.01401519775390625, 0.01390838623046875, 0.007541656494140625, 0.014678955078125, -0.0145721435546875, -0.013671875, -0.014434814453125, 0.00946807861328125, 0.0034961700439453125, 0.0183258056640625, -0.006496429443359375, 0.004547119140625, -0.00258636474609375, 0.01558685302734375, -0.01175689697265625, 0.003421783447265625, 0.0294952392578125, -0.008056640625, 0.0198516845703125, -0.0197601318359375, -0.0004482269287109375, 0.0214691162109375, -0.010955810546875, -0.0009756088256835938, 0.0010395050048828125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. In this study, \"skilled accounting judgement\" was operationalised by having professional accountants annotate each invoice according to a standardised set of GL coding guidelines, with ambiguous cases resolved by consensus. Inter-annotator agreement was measured on a subset of 1,000 invoices, yielding a Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.89, indicating high consistency. While AI is increasingly used to automate such tasks, including invoice categorisation, there is a research gap regarding the effectiveness and interpretability of in-house small language models (SLMs) compared to larger, external models. The main objective of this study is to evaluate whether in-house SLMs can provide accurate, secure, and interpretable invoice categorisation, and to understand the relationship between embedding geometry and classification performance in this domain.\n\n**Background and Definitions:**  \nRecent advances in natural language processing have enabled the use of sentence transformers and SLMs for document classification. In this context, \"anisotropic\" refers to an embedding space where data points are unevenly distributed, while \"isotropic clusters\" are locally dense, uniform groupings within that space. \"Structured input\" denotes invoice data reformatted with explicit fields and labels, as opposed to unstructured, free-text input.\n\n**Methodology:**  \nWe analysed a proprietary financial corpus of 12,000 invoices from multiple clients, each labelled with GL codes and vendor identities. Annotation was performed by certified accountants following a documented protocol, with periodic adjudication to ensure label quality. Preprocessing included anonymisation, tokenisation, and removal of non-informative fields. For model evaluation, we used 5-fold cross-validation with client-level splits: invoices from each client appeared in only one fold, ensuring that test data represented unseen clients and preventing data leakage. We evaluated two pre-trained models: a small sentence transformer (SBERT) and a classic SLM (DeBERTa). Both models were fine-tuned on a single NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU for 10 epochs, using a batch size of 32 and a learning rate of 2e-5. Baselines included a zero-shot large language model (LLM; GPT-3.5, prompted without fine-tuning) and a vendor identity baseline (assigning the most frequent GL code per vendor).\n\n**Geometric Analysis:**  \nTo assess embedding space geometry, we computed the isotropy coefficient (as in Ethayarajh, 2019) and visualised embeddings using t-SNE and UMAP. Clustering structure was quantified using silhouette scores and Davies-Bouldin indices. We observed that the sentence-embedding space is globally anisotropic (isotropy coefficient < 0.3) but contains locally isotropic clusters, with clusters strongly correlated to vendor identity (average silhouette score 0.62).\n\n**Results:**  \nSBERT fine-tuned on our dataset achieved an average accuracy of 0.96 (\u00b10.01) and F1 score of 0.94 (\u00b10.02), outperforming the zero-shot LLM (accuracy 0.85, F1 0.81) and the vendor identity baseline (accuracy 0.88, F1 0.83). McNemar\u2019s test indicated that SBERT\u2019s improvement over baselines was statistically significant (p < 0.01). Performance breakdown showed that SBERT improved classification for smaller, challenging categories (F1 0.87 for categories with <50 samples) and for new clients, reaching an F1 score of 0.90 (\u00b10.03) with approximately 100 client-specific invoices. Per-vendor analysis revealed consistent gains across both frequent and infrequent vendors. The structured input format, which included explicit field labels and tabular formatting, did not improve SLM performance compared to unstructured text (\u0394F1 < 0.01, not significant); error analysis suggested that the model\u2019s pre-training on natural language may limit its ability to leverage artificial structure, and that field labels sometimes introduced noise or redundancy.\n\n**Discussion and Limitations:**  \nOur findings indicate that in-house SLMs can achieve high accuracy and generalisation in invoice categorisation, with embedding geometry linked to classification performance. However, several limitations must be noted. First, the proprietary dataset may contain label noise despite high inter-annotator agreement, and class imbalance (with some GL codes underrepresented) could bias results. While stratified sampling was used, rare categories remain challenging. There is also a risk of model memorisation, particularly for vendors with many examples. The lack of external validation limits generalisability, and the structured input result may be model-specific. Regarding interpretability, we applied integrated gradients and attention visualisation to a subset of predictions, finding that models often relied on vendor names and key invoice terms; however, interpretability remains limited for complex or ambiguous cases.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nIn-house SLM implementations, such as fine-tuned SBERT, show promise for secure and interpretable invoice categorisation within the studied dataset, outperforming both zero-shot LLMs and simple vendor-based heuristics. Nonetheless, these findings are limited to our proprietary data and annotation protocol; further research is needed to confirm generalisability, address class imbalance and label noise, and improve interpretability across broader settings."}}